1
00:00:14,170 --> 00:00:17,830
1.4 components of the PIMBOC guide.

2
00:00:18,650 --> 00:00:23,126
Projects comprise several
key components that will

3
00:00:23,127 --> 00:00:26,710
effectively manage result in
their successful completion.

4
00:00:27,290 --> 00:00:31,390
This guide identifies and explains these
components.

5
00:00:32,210 --> 00:00:37,010
The various components interrelate to each
other during the management of a project.

6
00:00:37,810 --> 00:00:41,830
The key components are described briefly
in Table 13.

7
00:00:41,831 --> 00:00:47,290
These components are more fully explained
in the sections that follow the table.

8
00:00:48,050 --> 00:00:52,830
Please pay particular attention to this
diagram because you can see that the first

9
00:00:52,831 --> 00:00:57,106
entry mentions a series
of phases that the project

10
00:00:57,107 --> 00:01:01,890
passes through and the
second mentions project phases.

11
00:01:02,590 --> 00:01:06,952
However, the phases that
the project passes through

12
00:01:06,953 --> 00:01:11,130
and project phases are
two quite different things.

13
00:01:11,131 --> 00:01:13,990
They're not related despite the name.

14
00:01:14,370 --> 00:01:16,670
This catches a lot of people on the exam.

15
00:01:17,730 --> 00:01:23,850
The series of phases should really be
called the series of stages or similar

16
00:01:23,851 --> 00:01:31,044
because it means, for example, initiation
phase, planning phase and so on.

17
00:01:31,130 --> 00:01:36,210
But a project phase is more like a mini
project within the main project.

18
00:01:36,310 --> 00:01:41,570
For example, if you are managing a project
to build an office block, you might have a

19
00:01:41,571 --> 00:01:44,570
separate project phase to do the
landscaping.

20
00:01:45,270 --> 00:01:49,250
It's within the main project, but managed
as a separate project.

21
00:01:50,290 --> 00:01:54,310
Component one of the PIMBOC guide is the
project lifecycle.

22
00:01:54,970 --> 00:01:59,010
A project life cycle is
the series of phases that a

23
00:01:59,011 --> 00:02:02,291
project passes through
from its start to its completion.

24
00:02:02,590 --> 00:02:04,690
You need to know that for the examination.

25
00:02:04,850 --> 00:02:07,890
It provides the basic framework for
managing the project.

26
00:02:08,330 --> 00:02:14,950
This basic framework applies regardless of
the specific project work involved.

27
00:02:15,630 --> 00:02:19,030
The phases may be sequential, iterative,
i.e.

28
00:02:19,031 --> 00:02:22,453
you do them over and over
again, or overlapping, you may

29
00:02:22,454 --> 00:02:25,261
do two at the same time,
two or more at the same time.

30
00:02:25,790 --> 00:02:31,650
All projects can be mapped to the generic
lifecycle shown in figure 1,5,

31
00:02:31,950 --> 00:02:36,890
and please note that this generic life
cycle appears on the exam now and then,

32
00:02:37,130 --> 00:02:39,010
so you need to memorize that.

33
00:02:39,030 --> 00:02:40,370
It's quite a simple thing to learn.

34
00:02:40,390 --> 00:02:45,190
And along on this diagram, it's quite hard
to see the forest for the trees.

35
00:02:45,690 --> 00:02:48,930
The generic lifecycle is up at the top of
the diagram.

36
00:02:49,650 --> 00:02:54,710
And you can see it's one starting the
project, two, organizing and preparing,

37
00:02:55,190 --> 00:02:59,070
three, carrying out the work for end in
the project.

38
00:02:59,790 --> 00:03:06,150
And in this section on life cycles,
when you see the word phase, think of a

39
00:03:06,151 --> 00:03:09,070
stage such as those in the generic life
cycle.

40
00:03:09,071 --> 00:03:12,961
Development life cycles can be predictive,

41
00:03:12,962 --> 00:03:17,144
iterative, incremental,
adaptive or a hybrid model.

42
00:03:17,350 --> 00:03:18,550
What does that mean?

43
00:03:19,150 --> 00:03:22,627
In a predictive life cycle,
the project scope, time and

44
00:03:22,628 --> 00:03:27,170
cost are determined in the
early phases of the life cycle.

45
00:03:27,950 --> 00:03:33,670
The word predictive means being able to
predict or to see ahead because you're

46
00:03:33,671 --> 00:03:37,371
trying to predict everything in detail
right from the start of the project,

47
00:03:37,650 --> 00:03:38,630
which is the time.

48
00:03:39,070 --> 00:03:43,090
When you know the least about it,
it's also referred to as a waterfall life

49
00:03:43,091 --> 00:03:48,310
cycle because it's like walking down a
flight of stairs, but you can't go back up

50
00:03:48,311 --> 00:03:53,410
the stairs again, and so it's like taking
a canoe over a series of waterfalls.

51
00:03:54,330 --> 00:03:59,550
In an iterative life cycle, the project
scope is generally determined early in the

52
00:03:59,551 --> 00:04:04,690
project life cycle, but time and cost
estimates are routinely modified as the

53
00:04:04,691 --> 00:04:07,490
project team's understanding of the
product increases.

54
00:04:08,810 --> 00:04:13,530
Iterations develop the project through a
series of repeated cycles, while

55
00:04:13,531 --> 00:04:18,010
increments successfully add to the
functionality of the product.

56
00:04:18,850 --> 00:04:23,870
And so here you are acknowledging that
trying to predict everything in detail

57
00:04:23,871 --> 00:04:27,371
right from the start of the project,
where you know the least about it,

58
00:04:27,670 --> 00:04:29,770
is probably not the best way to do it.

59
00:04:30,090 --> 00:04:34,610
And so in an iterative life cycle,
you're treating the project more like an

60
00:04:34,611 --> 00:04:38,492
experiment, which in many
cases, especially in information

61
00:04:38,493 --> 00:04:41,990
technology projects,
that's exactly what it is.

62
00:04:42,390 --> 00:04:47,990
In an incremental lifecycle, the
deliverable is produced through a series

63
00:04:47,991 --> 00:04:53,904
of iterations that successfully add
functionality within a predetermined timeframe.

64
00:04:54,350 --> 00:04:58,090
The deliverable contains a
necessary and sufficient capability

65
00:04:58,091 --> 00:05:02,750
to be considered complete
only after the final iteration.

66
00:05:02,950 --> 00:05:05,190
This one reminds me of the old riddle.

67
00:05:05,310 --> 00:05:07,150
How can you eat a whole elephant?

68
00:05:07,890 --> 00:05:09,750
Answer, one bite at a time.

69
00:05:10,610 --> 00:05:13,610
Adaptive life cycles are agile,
iterative or incremental.

70
00:05:14,470 --> 00:05:20,230
The detail scope is defined and approved
before the start of one iteration.

71
00:05:21,150 --> 00:05:26,570
Adaptive life cycles are also referred to
as agile or change-driven life cycles.

72
00:05:27,230 --> 00:05:31,210
This type of life cycle is fast moving and
though it's far removed from the

73
00:05:31,211 --> 00:05:37,010
waterfall, say when you're managing an
adaptive life cycle, project, it can feel

74
00:05:37,011 --> 00:05:40,570
like you're in that little canoe
plummeting over a waterfall.

75
00:05:41,370 --> 00:05:46,570
A hybrid life cycle is a combination of
predictive and adaptive life cycle.

76
00:05:46,910 --> 00:05:50,873
These elements of the project
that are well known or have

77
00:05:50,874 --> 00:05:54,490
fixed requirements for a
predictive development life cycle.

78
00:05:55,170 --> 00:06:00,630
And those elements that are still evolving
follow an adaptive development lifecycle.

79
00:06:00,990 --> 00:06:04,505
It's up to the project management
team, not just the Project

80
00:06:04,506 --> 00:06:07,930
Manager to determine the
best lifecycle for each project.

81
00:06:08,610 --> 00:06:11,709
The project life-cycle needs
to be flexible enough to

82
00:06:11,710 --> 00:06:15,030
deal with the variety of
factors included in the project.

83
00:06:15,550 --> 00:06:20,870
Life cycle flexibility may be accomplished
by identifying the process or processes

84
00:06:20,871 --> 00:06:26,610
needed to be performed in each phase,
performing the process or processes

85
00:06:26,611 --> 00:06:31,970
identified in the appropriate phase,
adjusting the various attributes of a

86
00:06:31,971 --> 00:06:37,110
phase, the example name, duration,
exit criteria and entrance criteria.

87
00:06:38,050 --> 00:06:41,759
Project life cycles are
independent of product

88
00:06:41,760 --> 00:06:45,124
life cycles which may
be produced by a project.

89
00:06:45,770 --> 00:06:51,570
A product life cycle is a series of phases
that represent the evolution of the

90
00:06:51,571 --> 00:06:56,388
product from concept
through delivery, growth,

91
00:06:56,389 --> 00:07:01,130
maturity and to its eventual
end of life or retirement.

92
00:07:01,131 --> 00:07:07,030
For example, a project lifecycle,
to develop the first Concord supersonic

93
00:07:07,031 --> 00:07:13,844
aircraft took about eight years, but the
product life cycle was about 46 years.

94
00:07:13,930 --> 00:07:18,650
That's eight years of development,
plus 38 years until retired from service.

95
00:07:19,050 --> 00:07:23,610
However, I noticed recently that one
Concord has been bought by a group of

96
00:07:23,611 --> 00:07:28,450
businessmen, we're hoping to put it back
into service, so the product life cycle

97
00:07:28,451 --> 00:07:32,830
might end up a lot longer than the 46
years.

98
00:07:33,750 --> 00:07:34,583
Project phase.

99
00:07:35,450 --> 00:07:40,670
The Pimbook guide says a project phase is
a collection of logically related project

100
00:07:40,671 --> 00:07:46,150
activities that culminates in the
completion of one or more deliverables.

101
00:07:46,910 --> 00:07:51,530
The phases in a life cycle can be
described by a variety of attributes.

102
00:07:52,110 --> 00:07:56,110
Attributes may be measurable and unique to
a specific phase.

103
00:07:56,950 --> 00:08:03,130
Attributes may include the name of For
example, phase A, phase B, phase one,

104
00:08:03,330 --> 00:08:05,570
phase two, proposal phase.

105
00:08:06,230 --> 00:08:11,230
Numbering, e.g., three phases in the
project, five phases in the project.

106
00:08:11,710 --> 00:08:15,410
Duration, example, one week, one month,
one quarter.

107
00:08:16,570 --> 00:08:19,830
Resource requirements, e.g., people,
buildings, equipment.

108
00:08:20,750 --> 00:08:26,290
Entrance criteria for a project to move
into that phase, e.g., specified

109
00:08:26,291 --> 00:08:31,333
approvals, documents, specified
documents complete, and

110
00:08:31,334 --> 00:08:35,630
exit criteria for a project
to complete a phase, e.g.

111
00:08:36,550 --> 00:08:40,130
documented approvals, completed documents,
completed deliverables.

112
00:08:40,890 --> 00:08:45,470
Projects may be separated into distinct
phases or sub-components.

113
00:08:46,270 --> 00:08:49,119
These phases or
sub-components are generally given

114
00:08:49,120 --> 00:08:51,991
names that indicate the type
of work done in that phase.

115
